And above the house was like a-- a dog house. There was no entrance, but it was like a vent for the heat. The only way to get in there is climb up on the roof from the first house and sneak inside. And I felt this is going to be safe, because they cannot find me. In order to find me, they have to climb on the roof and this.

I don't know, but I went there. I waited till it was dark, climbed up, because I didn't want anybody to see, if there is any Christian or anybody else. Across the street there was a-- a church. And I didn't want anybody to see that I'm climbing in.

So I waited was-- till dark. I knew my sister was hiding somewhere with my-- with-- with my mother. My father went to my uncle. And here I'm by myself up there. And I'm freezing cold. It was, you know, February. I had no blankets, nothing.

5 o'clock in the morning-- I guessed right. 5 o'clock in the morning, this big van comes right in front of me, smack down. I could look down, I can see the van. Now, up till now, nobody knew what's happening. I mean, you heard stories, these-- I was looking straight down. I could see every little thing.

These police who came out quietly. They surrounded the houses. Went to the house, aroused all the-- the people, women, children, these, in-- in-- in nightgowns and pajamas, whatever this-- happened. Pushed them in, no possession, nothing. Kept beating with the butts, make room, make room, higher, higher.

Took children, small children, threw them inside, and screams. And-- because they were packed like sardines. I'm looking down, and I see this whole thing.

Among the Jews, I see my aunt, my cousin. I see those two Jews who are working for the Germans shown the-- the papers. The guy takes it, rips it up, apart. Forget about it Get in there.

After it was all packed, and there was no more room, I mean, really, I seen those tailgates closed, the tarpaulin pulled down. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] and the engine of this van start up, and they're erasing them, I mean, erasing them. Of course, at that time, I didn't know anything about carbon monoxide. I never knew about gas. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

And I hear screams, screams, screams, for about 10 minutes, and then I don't hear a thing. Now, I was the only one to witness this thing, because nobody-- I had a vantage point, being on top. And this was right in front of me. And the truck took off.

INT: Did you know then what had happened?

MS: No. I-- I said, even in normal circumstances, you couldn't survive, because no air. There's no windows, no air, was-- was tight van, closed up, and on top of it, they put a tarpaulin on it. So there was-- there was no way anybody could survive without any gas. Of course later on I found they were pumping carbon monoxide into the van. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

While this was happening here, my father was hiding my uncle. Another van took him and my uncles and aunts and cousin. Had I gone with my father, not changed my mind, I wouldn't be here to tell you that. So after it was all over, I looked around. I went over to see, and I was told that my father and the rest of them were taken.

I figured the-- the same thing, same fate happened to them. And I looked, I said, what happened to my mother? They says, fortunately for them, they were safe. And this was going on every single day. And I just I went to the Judenrate, and I told them what I have witnessed.

They say, yeah, we heard about it. It was because I didn't have to tell them that there was gas. I told them that, you know, I don't how the people survived, because it was tight packed. [INAUDIBLE]. And every single day, little by little, the whole Jewish population of the whole town was taken.

Finally, one day, the remaining Jews, you all report to the church, the Polish church. The biggest Polish church there. Everybody, every Jews, just move them. You-- by 10 o'clock, if you're not there, we find you outside, you're shot. So the remaining Jews from my hometown, women and children and-- and everybody, stayed in the church.

And then they called everybody out and had a selection, men on one side, women on other side, children. And they looked over. The Germans looked over, and-- and anybody was asked who is a tailor, who is a shoemaker, who is a carpenter, who is a mechanic. You know, they had-- because on their ID card, when you went to the police station to sign up, you'd specify what kind of occupation you had. And you had to show-- you--

INT: How-- how many people were gathered at the end?

MS: It couldn't have been more than maybe 500 at the most. So they picked-- you showed your ID card. Carpenters here. Look them over, if he's young enough. Even if he's a carpenter, if you're old, they don't take you. Separate them.

Women and children automatically stay in the church. Because they figured the fate, the-- the van's going to come and take them away. So out of the 500, maybe selected about 200. OK?

INT: Where were you in this selection?

MS: I was-- I was-- I was there. And I-- and I was waiting around for the first chance to sneak in the people who were selected to go to-- to-- to the work. I was waiting among the crowd. When these people marched off to the train station, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] I snuck in. My mother was selected, and my-- my sister, and I snuck with them. We were marched to the train station, put on a train, and shipped to Lódz ghetto, which is the nearest, biggest town.

INT: What kind of train did you travel on?

MS: It-- surprisingly, that was a-- a-- a civilian train.

INT: A civilian train?

MS: Yeah, yeah. And of course, there were-- remained there my aunt, the one picture I showed you here-- I'll show later on, she did-- all the women and children remained in-- in-- in-- in the-- the church. Their fate, I assume, what's happened, take them to the vans. And we traveled to the Lódz ghetto, which was established, I think, 1940-- wires-- were brought in there to the Lódz ghetto.

INT: All right. We're going to come to the end of this tape now.

Tell me about the Lódz ghetto.

MS: As we were saying is once we arrived to Lódz ghetto, which is maybe a an hour away from our hometown, we are-- Jewish policemen from Lodz ghetto came to meet the train. Told everybody to get off the train, march us to the gate, which has a gate. The gate was a German policeman watching the gate on one side and a Jewish policeman other side, went through it.

And we-- there was some housing for us, for, you know. We were not the only ones coming from the surrounding areas. It seemed that all the surrounding areas in Poland, besides our hometown, all the Jews were assembled and brought into the Lódz ghetto.

INT: What date was this? Do you remember?

MS: That was, I think, the summer of 19-- this is right after the hanging, I would say. This was in February. This had to be about June or July, the same year.

INT: What year was that?

MS: 1942. We arrived in the ghetto. And they gave us some temporary housing. We had to live two, three families in a-- in a-- actually, let's see, how-- one, two-- we shared one big room with three families.

INT: Who was with you at this time, of your family?

MS: My mother and my sister, because my father was already taken in those vans.

INT: How about aunts or uncles or cousins?

MS: They were all-- I-- my-- my one aunt and my cousin I saw go in the van. My-- one of my aunts and the children remained in the church. And I know what their fate was.

INT: How about your grandparents?

MS: My-- my grandparents, unfortunately, they died prior to the-- the war. So I had no grandparents when the war started. And one of my uncles, which I have a picture here of, he was taken, of course, to the Lódz ghetto. His wife and the children were not allowed to go, and she left-- remained in the church. And of course I know what their fate was.

So we were put together with two strange families, share one room, not even as big as the one here. So we did, like, put blankets with a-- with a rope to separate.

INT: What were your sleeping accommodations?

MS: On the floor, mostly. We found some-- some sacks with straw. There was no room for beds. There was no sanitary facilities. Water, we had to bring up from-- from-- there was a pump on the-- on the-- on the first floor.

INT: Was there electricity?

MS: Yeah, there was electricity. But there's a outhouse. And there was--

INT: What about food?

MS: Well, we were given ration cards. And then we were told to report for work. I was sent to work in a factory, a metal factory, where they were making-- what do you call it-- taps for Germans' soldiers' shoes.

Now, what happens is see, people don't realize. You know only about Schindler. But in the Lódz ghetto, there was about 20 Schindlers. They came to the Lódz ghetto, they-- they set up factories, and they paid very-- practically nothing.

INT: These were civilians?

MS: These were civilian Germans who set up factories making uniforms for the German soldiers, making shoes. Towards the end, they were making ammunition. They were making leather coats, women's coats. These are private Germans who had a chance to make a fortune of money, didn't have to pay any labor.

All they did in exchange, go to the local farmers, buy up some turnips or potatoes. And if you worked-- you had to show-- you had a card. When you worked, you got a ration every two weeks of maybe a couple of pounds of turnips, a couple of potatoes.

And on the day you worked, you came to the-- to the factory, you-- you-- you put your-- your ID card, and you were entitled to get a soup. You worked eight hours-- after four hours, lunchtime, you get a-- a-- a bowl of soup. And the potatoes, two pieces of potatoes were counted in. That was your meal.

INT: Bread?

MS: No bread. The bread, when you get a ration, let's say one bread for two weeks, a-- a-- a-- maybe two pounds, three pounds of bread. And you have to ration yourself. You were hungry. You were dying. But you knew very well that you had-- this had to last you for-- for so long. And if you didn't, most people who couldn't control themselves, they died from hunger. So--

INT: Did-- did people share or help each other in any way at this time?

MS: How could they? Everybody was got the same ration. Everybody get the same piece of bread, everybody the same amount of turnips. You know what I mean? And the only thing is if you could work, you weren't sick, you went to work, and you got the soup. If you didn't go to work, you didn't get that soup.

So even if you were sick, you forced yourself to go to work. Because in order to survive, you needed a little hot-- a little soup, which was-- which is three piece potatoes.

INT: Did your mother and your sister work also?

MS: All of them worked, yes.

INT: What did they do?

MS: They worked in the-- the factories.

INT: Same factory?

MS: Same factory. They were carrying stuff. As a matter of fact, we-- the finished products she has to carry to-- to the warehouse, the women did, and they put it on-- on-- on the trucks, and were shipped to Germany.

In order-- in order for-- for-- for us to survive, I volunteered to work two shifts. That means I left in the morning, 6 o'clock in the morning, to walk to the factory. And I was working until about 5 o'clock. You got a soup. If you want another-- another shift, from 5:00 to 11:00, you got another soup. So I worked for two shifts.

INT: How old were you then?

MS: Let's see. This was 19-- in 1942. Let's see. I was 13, 14 years old. Because I was two and shy of-- the-- the war broke out, I was two-- two years shy of 11. That was 1939. So it's about.

So when I worked those two shifts, my mother and my sister could share my ration for dinner, which was very meager. And that-- and that way, they had something extra to eat. And I survived with two-- two bowls of soup. But I worked all day. Come home 11 o'clock at night.

INT: At work, who worked with you?

MS: Oh, a lot of Jews, all Jews.

INT: And the supervisors?

MS: The supervisor, it was a-- it was a-- a-- a German civilian, like, like a Schindler. Used to come in every week, go around, watch everybody's doing their work, examine some of the products.

INT: Did you ever receive any salary for this work?

MS: They got a German-- Yiddish money, paper money, which means you got-- which is worthless. But it was good enough for you to-- to buy the-- to pay for the-- the rations. When you get a piece of bread, you have to pay for it.

INT: You had to pay for that?

MS: Yeah. We would pay, but Jewish money printed in a-- you know, in a-- if you work so many hours, the end of the week, you got paper money. It was worthless. You couldn't buy anything except for the rations, for the store.

INT: Did you have clothing, extra clothing or anything?

MS: No.

INT: Were you able to take anything with you?

MS: We-- no, absolutely. What you did is whatever we brought with us. And-- and what happened was you-- you had a tablecloth, you made something out of it, jacket, any kind of thing you-- you find to-- to wear clothes. Because there was no material or anything like that.

INT: Were there Jewish police in the Lódz ghetto?

MS: Yes.

INT: Was there a Judenrate there also?

MS: Yes. Of course. And they-- of course they-- they supervised the whole thing. OK. On those conditions, you could not survive very long. So every-- you see-- you pass by in the morning, you see dead bodies all over the place. People who just died from hunger, for sicknesses. You know what I mean?

INT: Was there much sickness in the ghetto?

MS: Of course, of course. I mean, but there's no medication. There was nothing. One day, I was thirsty, and I was drinking-- the water was contaminated water.

And I became yellow. I had yellow jaundice. I became yellow. I'm yellow. That you-- you see like this, this here, dark yellow, pain. Couldn't go to work, constant pain, no medication. I was laying in bed for a day or two, temperature, and I was ready to die. I just figured any day now, you know what I mean? Because I couldn't endure the pain.

I don't know, a miracle. After about three or four days, I was coming to myself. And-- and-- and of course, my mother and my sister, they-- they cut their rations, and they-- what they did is-- is they turned in some of the rations for sugar. I don't know. Somebody told them that sugar does you good for-- for yellow jaundice.

So they traded my ration of bread, and they got for sugar, and they feed me sugar. And I survived. But I was still skinny, sickly. And the Germans used to come in at least once a month or twice a month. Every Jew has to appear down in the-- on the front of the building. They made selections.

If you look sickly, you look skinny, they put you out in a truck. At that time, it was a wagon with horses. And they make selection. Of course they looked at me, and I was look sickly and everything else. Pointed me to the wagon.

On the wagon was old people, small children. Common sense told me where they're going to take them is not going to be any good. I have to do something drastic. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] So as-- as the general pointed me out to the-- to get on top of the-- of the wagon, he didn't look. I turned away to hide. He noticed me. He went over with his gun. He-- and hit me over right here.

INT: On the back of your head.

MS: Yeah, and I became unconscious. When I became unconscious-- semiconscious, but I knew what is happening. And I was bleeding. These two Jewish policemen took me and threw me up on that wagon. But I-- I gained some conscious, and I played that I was sick. Because on the wagon, there was a Jewish policeman who's supposed to watch us, that nobody--

INT: Jumps off.

MS: Jumps off. As-- as they picked up speed, and-- and-- and we're going, and the Jewish policeman looked up for a minute, I jumped off, knowing fully well he had no gun. He's not going to stop the wagon. Because if-- if he chases me, the other people, whoever, I don't know how many, could have jumped off. So I just ran.

INT: Where were you at this time? Still in the ghetto?

MS: Yeah. That's in the ghetto. Because--

INT: It was in the ghetto?

MS: They had selection-- selection in the ghettos, you know what I mean? And I was bleeding. And my mother and my sister were standing there and saw me, that I was in the wagon. They figure I'm gone. And I-- I-- I hid for a-- for a-- for a couple hours and eventually returned to home. So I saved myself.

And this was going on for a-- from 1942 till '43, when the-- they started eliminating the ghetto, section by section, from the-- you know, they-- they put all the Jews closer and closer together, and they told you you have to appear in-- in front of the train station. And when you go to the train station, everybody's going to get a bread.

People being hungry, they flocked. You-- we're going to send you someplace to-- to work, and you're going to get bread. You're going to get food. You know. [INAUDIBLE].

INT: Did you hear at any-- at this time about what was happening to the-- these transports and Jews? When we change the tape, we will continue on with this.

MS: OK.

