INT: What were you hearing about the fate of the Jews in Europe at this time or in Poland?

MS: We had no idea.

INT: In 1943?

MS: 1943, all I-- all I know is that they were eliminating the ghetto. Little by little, they condensed all the streets where the Jews living in the ghetto, move from one section closer and closer to us.

INT: But when they were deporting these people, you had no idea what was happening to them?

MS: No. Well, we were-- we were told that the resettlement to work-- to go to Germany to work, rumors. They even told the tales to-- to bring the-- their sewing machine heads with them. All the-- the craftsmen, the-- the carpenters should bring their-- their tools, because, you know, big disguise. Of course at that time, we thought that's what will happen.

INT: I would like to talk a little bit about the conditions in-- in the ghetto during the-- this time, before we-- we leave it.

MS: Yeah. Yeah.

INT: What was the general feeling? Was there a-- what-- was there a terrible feeling of despair, or was there hope?

MS: No. The main emphasis was to survive each day. We didn't know what's going to happen.

INT: Was there-- were there any religious practices going on in the ghetto?

MS: Yeah. There was some religious, yeah, practices. Actually, matter of fact, there was a Jewish-- Yiddish theater. There was musicians. They have some cultural things going on on a small scale.

INT: Was there a newspaper of any kind?

MS: I-- I-- I-- I'm-- I assumed there was a newspaper, but I didn't-- we were-- I didn't actually look into it. But I'm sure there was a-- a-- a publishing newspaper, yeah. And there was-- there was some orphanages, which some teachers organized some orphanages because when the parents died in the ghetto, to take care of some of these orphanages.

INT: Were there ever any acts of resistance in the ghetto?

MS: There was no way. If-- matter of fact, they were even hanging people, because if-- so if some of these factories-- I remember one-- one time, there was a-- a leather goods factory, and one-- one-- one German accused that these, the Jews, sabotaged some machine. And they took him, and-- and they hung him, just a warning.

I don't-- I don't think there was any proof that he did it, but the machine broke down, and they accused him. So there was no resistance. No way.

INT: Did people trying to escape from the ghetto?

MS: I-- the possibility of escaping from the ghetto was one nil. There was high wire. That was guarded 24 hours by the-- on one side from the-- from the German police. And the inside is the Jewish police.

And I-- I doubt it very much. I don't-- I wouldn't know that, because this was the least on my mind. To me, it was [INAUDIBLE] how to survive the day, get enough food to survive an extra day. So--

INT: And you mentioned that there was a theater and music.

MS: Yeah.

INT: Did you ever attend any of this?

MS: That was only for-- for the-- for the privileged people, you know, the one who are--

INT: Was there a class distinction within the ghetto?

MS: Yeah, yeah, yeah, of course. The-- the policemen got extra rations. The firemen, Jewish firemen, who-- who were watching for fires from these Schindlers-- you know, of course they had invested-- they brought machinery from Germany, and they want to make sure it's safe. So they had in each factory, had Jewish firemen watching that shouldn't fire break out. For that, they got an additional ration of food. So that was a privileged class.

INT: So life was easier for them, somewhat?

MS: Easier in the sense that they got extra food, the policemen and the firemen.

INT: And so were these the people that partook of the social--

MS: So if there-- if there is any concerts or-- or-- or theater, they're the ones who got the tickets, because they-- they worked, you know, together. They were the privileged-- the privileged ones. And the ones who worked for the-- for the Judenrate, you know. I mean, they-- they got the privileges.

INT: You also mentioned that there were many deaths.

MS: Yeah.

INT: Natural deaths.

MS: Oh yeah. Most-- most of them, if you-- you look at those bodies, they were practically skeletons.

INT: What-- what happened to these bodies?

MS: They had the-- the-- yeah. These people who buried them got extra rations too. Because you only got extra-- extra food if you volunteered to go to the-- to the-- to the cemetery and dig graves in the-- in the coldest day, which was the-- the earth was so frozen, which was-- under normal circumstances, it's-- it's-- it's hard work, because on the food you got, you had no energy.

There was no meat or any sort of-- so only-- only turnips and potatoes and-- and bread, no anything strong to dig graves. And they couldn't keep up with the-- with the dead. So if you volunteered to dig graves, you had extra ration. My uncle, who was fairly physical, you know, before the war, he volunteered, so he'd get an extra piece of bread or extra soup, to-- to work on this-- the-- the graves.

INT: When-- when they had these burials, were-- was there a Jewish ceremony of any kind?

MS: There was-- they were-- they were-- they were digging long pits, not individual graves. And they had some rabbis performing a prayer. These wagons were pulled by-- by men, and they put five, six, seven bodies to-- pass by each street, and they see bodies on the floor, just pick them up. Or if somebody died in your family, you called them up. They come pick it up. Because they're afraid because some disease or-- or-- or you know, all-- all kind of diseases.

They picked them up, put them on the wagon, brought them to the cemetery. Wagon after wagon, they pushed them down the pits, and-- and the rabbis made some prayers, and they--

INT: Covered.

MS: They covered them up.

INT: All right. So let's get back now to the-- the time of the selections.

MS: So after we were all condensed, I guess the-- the war must have turned. But it was unknown to us that the Russian offensive started. So these Germans, first we had to dismantle all the factories, because the Germans, the Schindlers, so-called Schindlers, who invested, brought that machinery to the Poles, they wanted to [INAUDIBLE] and take it back to Germany, because they knew that the end is coming up. The end is coming. But something was happening. We knew something--

INT: The war-- the war was coming closer.

MS: Well, we had no-- no way of knowing. Why should they do it? So there was no use for us anymore. That means no use for us because-- eliminate the ghetto. The only reason we survived that long, as long we're useful for them to work. They were making money, and we were labor.

And of course they had selections. If you couldn't work anymore, they-- they send you away. So--

INT: Did people work outside of the ghetto also? Were people taken out of the ghetto to work?

MS: No, no, no, no, no, no. Everything was together. The-- the ghetto was all factories. From-- you name it, from-- from children's clothes to-- to-- to pillows to mattresses, all done for the civilian market.

INT: So nobody left the ghetto. The ghetto was contained.

MS: No, no. outside. There's no way you can get it done. Anyway, after they condensed all the Jews, we had to move from one part of the city, closer, closer together, so that they could get us all in one time. And then they said, look, you get no more rations. If you want to-- if you volunteer, you go to the train, you get a-- a bread, which is maybe two, three pounds. And being that everybody's hungry, they flocked.

You go into resettlement, you go to Germany to work, stuff like that, then people believed that. You wanted to believe, maybe you didn't want to believe, you had no choice. You didn't go today, you go tomorrow.

So finally, my mother and my sister and myself, we see everybody going. They're going to say this is going to take a month, and there-- there will be no Jew left here. So might as well get a head start or whatever it is, because we were hungry.

We had no more rations. There was no cards anymore. And we were starved. Sure enough, we go into these cattle trains. And everybody was given a bread.

INT: How long were you kept in the gathering place?

MS: Oh, it-- we just-- we just walked to the train. They didn't keep you. Just put you on cattle car, one train after another came in. And they just put you up, so many in a cattle train. They have a-- a big-- what do you call it? Barrel, whatever it is, to eliminate yourself. You know, the sanitary conditions.

Like I tell you, there's no-- there's no mattresses or straws. You just sit down. And if you're lucky enough, you sit the place don't get up, because you get up, there will be no place, because they were so tightly put in.

INT: About how--

MS: Women and children.

INT: About how many people were on a-- on a car?

MS: [SIGHS] I-- I would say maybe close to 100, maybe less. I couldn't tell you. But we were packed like sardines.

INT: Was the car open or closed?

MS: After we all--

INT: I mean, the roof. Was there--

MS: Oh, everything was closed. It had a couple, about two little windows. They were wired. So you could look outside. That's it-- no air, nothing. These are-- they were just-- yeah.

INT: Did they tell you where you were going?

MS: No. Absolutely no. Of course, the rumors was that we were being resettled. We're going to work to Germany or whatever. I mean, that's all false. Of course some people believed it.

INT: How long was your journey?

MS: [SIGHS] I-- I would say at least two days or more.

INT: Did you-- did they stop to give you food?

MS: Oh, of course not. They never open up-- once they sealed the cattle, we took off, we never stopped for one minute. Just to-- when the-- after about two days, the-- the-- the train slowed down. We looked out through the window, somebody was tall enough to-- to look out the window. So we see we're passing countryside. We didn't know where we're going.

Then we finally see, as one of them saw a sign that says Oswiecim. Oswiecim in Polish means Auschwitz. But we didn't know what Auschwitz was. That's all we knew.

And then the train stopped. And they-- they unhooked the locomotive, and another train came in and took us further in. And about, I would say about 6 o'clock in the morning, they opened up the cattle cars, everybody out. You know what I mean?

INT: Did you have any belongings with you at this time?

MS: Whatever-- whatever we took with us, had to leave. Everything you leave is going to follow you. You know, prior to going to-- to-- to the train, they told us, make sure you put your names on, you're writing down, because this way you-- you-- in case you're separated, you know who's who.

Anyway, we got off the train. We lined up. We see this-- these-- these prisoners-- prisoners with stripes, with hats and stripes.

INT: Did they say anything to you?

MS: Absolutely nothing. Because they were watched by the Germans constantly, they shouldn't utter a word. Except asking questions, they wouldn't say nothing. Just get out. Beat with sticks. Hurry up! Hurry up! You know, run. People beat you over the head. Just get up in the line. Because some people were older, couldn't move. They were just trampled up.

And on one side here, on the-- on the right side, we see ambulances with a Red Cross, nurses with white hats, everything else. Of course and-- and they were told, you know, this is for the elderly and children and sick people. I mean, it looked authentic, like a Red Cross. Right?

Then we wind up on the line. And we see this-- right in front, we see this German. Good-looking German, you know. Of course we didn't know at that time that was Mengele. Lined up.

And I remember, my mother-- I don't know how, she had her lipstick. And she went over me, and she put some of this on my cheek, I should look like--

INT: Healthy.

MS: Healthy. And then she said to-- to me, I should kiss my sister. I don't know if she had a premonition or something. Because she knew we're going to be separated.

Because she's seen in front of her that the women were sent to one side, the men the other side, that there's going to be separation. Of course, we didn't know about separation.

So there was a women camp here. There's a men's camp here. The ones who actually sent to the left, that meant they still survived. The ones who went to right--

INT: When you approached--

MS: Yeah.

INT: When you approached this--

MS: Mengele.

INT: German who was Mengele--

MS: Yeah. He pointed. He pointed the finger.

INT: He did. You--

MS: Yeah.

INT: You-- he made the selection.

MS: Yeah. Yeah. We had a-- he had like riding stick, a baton. Left, right, left, right. And there were soldiers waiting on both sides with-- with vicious dogs. And of course, the women with children went automatically to the-- to the-- to the right. And the other--

INT: Did anybody go on these ambulances? Did they put anybody--

MS: Oh, there's some old people who couldn't walk. You know, they helped them [INAUDIBLE].

INT: So they did take them?

MS: Yeah, yeah. So when I got in front of him, he looked me over, and he pointed to-- to the left. Of course I didn't know what was left, right, where everybody is going. So I went to-- to the left, to the men's camp. And then I saw my sister and my mother to the women's camp, so in other words, they survived that time, did not go to the-- because we--

INT: Were you able to speak to them at this point?

MS: No, no. You just saw them and-- and-- because I was-- just waved a little bit after going. And of course, we were pushed, go, hurry up. And once we-- we-- we were-- we were selected to go to the other camp, they-- they pushed it over there, soldier and everything else, undress. Take all clothes off, everything else. The only thing, you saved your shoes.

And you went through a-- a disinfection--

INT: Shower.

MS: --place. In other words, they sprayed you with some kind of chemicals. Water and spray, they were all over your body. You only had your shoes, nothing else. You came out the other side. They gave you some-- some striped clothes.

INT: You did not get your own clothing back?

MS: No, no. You left that behind. The only thing you had, your-- your shoes. And then after they gave us the-- the clothes, the-- they put you in those big wooden barracks. There were no beds, no nothing. This was-- this was-- this was Birkenau.

INT: Did they shave you?

MS: No, no. This was Birkenau. This was the-- the-- all the Jews who came to Auschwitz went to Birkenau. This was. Of course, far away, we see a big chimney, spewing off smoke, smelly odor.

INT: Did you know what it-- this was?

MS: No. Absolutely not. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And we went to this barrack, with big, giant barracks. And there was nothing to sit down. Lie down on the floor. Everybody sit down on the floor. And this German comes in with a-- a kapo, which is a-- which is a Jewish policeman, so-called. I-- he wasn't Jewish, actually, he was German.

Anybody who has any valuables. We said, what kind of valuables? We have no clothes. The only thing we had, shoes. If you swallowed any valuables, if we take x-rays or find something that-- that you hid something, you will be shot, or if you have anything hidden anyplace. But we had not-- we had-- we had nothing.

And if you have something, and you voluntarily give it to us, you get a extra bread. If we find that you hid someplace, we take it away, and you'll be shot. Sure enough, there was a couple volunteers. They had those false heels that opened up, and they had some-- some had the diamonds, some had some gold. And they turned it over.

They were very polite, these Germans. I mean, I don't think it was really the Germans. I think it was just a-- the kapos, the ones in the camps, they were-- they were hoarding the gold for themselves.

INT: They were-- they were prisoners themselves.

MS: Yeah, yeah. They-- they share. And they gave him a bread.

INT: They did give them.

MS: Yeah, yeah. So in other words, to entice the other ones who have-- if somebody hesitated, you give this, you get a bread. OK. And then, of course, you're afraid if they find out anyway, you'll be shot. And we stand there two days.

INT: Did they give you any food at that point?

MS: No. You got-- you got a-- a-- a bowl, those round bowls, dirty-looking bowls. And this was your life support-- no spoons. And you lined up. And the next morning, they put some watery soup in the-- in the bowl and gave us a tiny piece of bread. This was the meal for the day.

And you mingled around this big-- it's like a holding camp. Did you ever hear about if you keep cattle for the slaughterhouse, you keep them in a-- in a corral?

INT: Corral, mhm.

MS: You see, languages, I came in contact with-- here I am, 13 years old, 14 years old, I see grownup men, young ones, old ones, speaking all kind of foreign languages. I had no idea what they were talking about. You know, I mean, it was brand new to me. I had nobody. I didn't know anybody. [SIGHS]

For about two days, no-- no facilities, nothing. And one day, they call us out. It's this old, young teenagers, lined up, including myself lined up on the side. And this German went-- went around and looked us over.

And there's a table. And then they picked-- selected about, I would say, 200 or 300 of-- of us. And we came to the table. And the guy says, pull up your arm. And the tattooed with a-- with a pen, number. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] So-- so I-- I mentioned something to the-- some prisoners, [INAUDIBLE]. You think we should get the number? Stand in line?

He says, look, if you-- if they give you a number, that means you're going-- they're going to want to keep you alive yet. If they don't need you anymore, they're going to kill you or put you away-- or-- or before we came to it. So I asked them, I asked-- I asked one of the prisoners the day before. I says, what's this chimney?

So he says, did you come with any relatives. I said, yes. Which direction did they go? I said, they went to the-- to the left. They are still alive. I says, why? See, the ones that went to the-- to the right, they're smoke. In 10 minutes, you're-- they're not going to be alive. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

So I got a pretty good idea what was happening. So I came to the table. They gave me the [? two. ?] And they said line up. And we were going on a march. Where we were going, of course we didn't know where we were going.

Out of the gate-- and this is Birkenau-- two miles to the main camp in Auschwitz, which if you-- if your-- if they want to save you, you went to the main camp, and then you worked there, because you were still able-- able-bodied. If you remained-- if you remained in Birkenau, and they run out of trains, they took the prisoner from here, and they sent them to the gas chambers. So--

INT: So Birkenau was where--

MS: So in other words--

INT: --were-- was where the ovens were.

MS: Every-- now, Birkenau was where the ovens were at. Everybody from all over East Europe came to Birkenau. This was the selection. That where Mengele was. He made selections.

If you decided to keep you alive, from Birkenau, took you out to the other camp, the main camp, which was built-- compared, this was wooden barracks. Over there it was brick barracks. It was already a different kind of camp. You had a bunk.

INT: What kind of work did you do in Auschwitz?

MS: OK. So I-- of course I didn't know what's going to happening. They marched us to Birkenau. They put us up in barracks. I mean, this looked decent compared with over there, you sleep on the floor. They had bunk beds-- of course four across, three layers.

INT: Was there a mattress?

MS: Straw. Yeah. It was-- it was a-- a sack of straw-- no mattress.

INT: Blanket?

MS: There was-- yeah. There was one blanket. And there's a-- a-- what do you call it? The-- the man who was in charge, which is a German-- a German prisoner, because those German prisoners, they had the charge of the camp. There could have been--

INT: Was that a blockalteste?

MS: Yeah, yeah. He-- he could've been a-- I found out later on, he had a-- a black triangle, which meant that he was a murderer.

INT: What--

MS: And he was in charge of us.

INT: What other kind of prisoners were at this camp in Auschwitz? You mentioned the black. What did you have as a Jew?

MS: I had yellow. Jew had yellow. And the red-- red meant a-- a Christian Pole who was a political prisoner, called them. Then they had a-- a black, which was a murderer. And they had one for Gypsies. And they had a pink for homosexuals.

INT: Were these prisoners treated differently from Jews in any way?

MS: They would-- they would get-- they got the best jobs, meaning they-- they worked in a-- a better factories. They-- they worked in-- in a-- you know supervisors. You know, Jews were on the-- last one on the totem pole. In other words, do the dirty work.

INT: Did-- did they get more food?

MS: The Poles? They had connections, because the Poles sometimes managed to work in the kitchens. They-- some Pole civilians from Auschwitz used to come to-- to the camps to do some electrician, stuff like that. And somehow, they-- they smuggled some bread for these Poles.

INT: So there were civilians that came into the camps?

MS: They-- on the outside the camp, they were building facilities for the Germans, like-- like buildings for the-- for the German soldiers, the facilities, bath houses.

INT: Administrative buildings?

MS: Buildings for to-- to house the Germans with their families. There were builders, bricklayers, whatever it is. And somehow or other managed to-- to smuggle some bread for these-- for these Poles.

INT: So they-- so they were treated a little differently.

MS: Yeah, right, right. Now so we've got this barrack. Next morning, they told us we're going to extend-- we-- we need more facilities in Auschwitz to house more prisoners. We're going to teach you to be a bricklayer. School? Bricklayer? Sounds-- sounds perfect, right?

We go this-- to this place, and there is a Polish supervisor. He speaks Polish. He says-- and he starts as here's bricks. Here's this and that. And he started-- you are going to stand here till you're-- a couple of weeks, you should be able to-- to learn--

INT: Learn.

MS: --learn bricks. OK.

So we're standing there, you know. And then he teaches us, and we start putting bricks together and everything else, learn. Because they had-- we saw this open area. They-- they expected all the Jews from eastern-- from the rest of-- the Germans have planned to take all the Jews from Europe, eventually house them or bring them to Auschwitz, and they'll need more facilities.

So we have to build--

INT: More barracks.

MS: There was a clear-- clearing down in the forest, and building. And they needed people to lay the bricks into those barracks. So they used us.

This went on for about two, three weeks. I-- I think this was [SIGHS] till about-- towards the end of '44. All of a sudden, one day, no more school.

INT: 1944, you arrived in '43.

MS: Yeah. Yeah. So '44, the war-- the war, of course, the Russian were getting-- unknown to us, the Germans were losing. Here they're-- they're making plans to build-- see, these-- these German who were in charge of the camp didn't know what going on in the rest of the war. So when they must've found out that we are-- they might-- the Germans are losing the war, why would they train somebody to-- to-- to build bricks there, because they eliminated the camp. No more school.

What kind of work do? They sent me to work in a laundry. In a laundry which was washing the-- the German guards' uniform.

INT: All right. I'd like to talk a little bit about the physical conditions in Auschwitz. Was there a fence around the camp?

MS: Oh, an electrified fence. You get up in the morning, and you see bodies on the fence. Some guy-- some people who couldn't take the conditions just touched the wire, and-- and they died.

INT: So there were suicides?

MS: Yes.

INT: Yes.

MS: And especially-- and of course the-- they had selections every-- every while. These people we used to call Muselmann, which the-- become skeletons. That's-- in German, it's-- it's Muselmann. So they just take them in a selection, put them in a truck, and you'll never see them again. We knew where-- where they're going.

INT: Were there appells?

MS: Pardon?

INT: Appell? Zahlappells?

MS: Oh, every morning, I think about 5 o'clock in the morning, everybody had to stand outside, and they counted to make sure that every prisoner is accounted for. This was twice a day, in the morning and at night.

INT: How long did an appell take?

MS: Depending. If-- if everybody was accounted for, it might take maybe a half an hour. If there is somebody is missing or somebody's sick, it could take some two, three hours. Some prisoner fell asleep, never came out.

I remember one day, it was a cold day. We must have stayed about two hours, freezing. Freezing. You know, cold, the Polish winter, and all you had is this-- this-- this striped uniform, and that's it. And here, they're looking everybody barrack, every nook and corner, till they found him. Finally found him, you know, they shot him.

INT: They shot him.

MS: Yeah. So--

INT: What other forms of punishment did you witness in Auschwitz?

MS: Well, the-- of course the-- the-- the blockalteste, which is-- which is, most of them, German criminals, who is-- who committed some crime in Germany, they were in charge of the prisoners. So they're sadistic. They just, for no reason, they-- in the morning, they didn't wake you up. They came and put the light on and brought out a stick. They hit you right in the bed, for no reason, just to wake you up.

So everybody scrambled down from the bed and-- and-- and get out for appell. And-- and they walked around. They-- they-- they were so sadistic, they used to hang up prisoners by their arms, on-- on the-- on the-- on the back-- poles, you know what I mean? Just for no reason. To have fun.

INT: And you witnessed this?

MS: Of course.

INT: OK. Was there ever any time when a whole group of people was punished for the act of maybe one or two people?

MS: [SIGHS] Oh, this is funny. In 19-- in 1944, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] one day he said, nobody is going to work. What is happening? We're going to have a commission come in to-- inspectors coming to camp. Everybody had to scrub the barracks. Everybody had to be clean, make sure their uniform is-- is washed and cleaned.

The-- the-- the-- it has to be scrubbed. I mean, scrubbed, really, the front and the back. Everybody had to look their nicest. I mean, hair. People have to shave and everything else. What kind of inspection? And what-- oh. And they said they'd give us soccer balls.

INT: Soccer balls?

MS: Yes. To-- to play games, like-- like a-- like a picnic. We didn't know what was happening. About 12, 1 o'clock, there's Germans coming in with civilians. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And we found out there's a Swiss Red Cross representative came in to inspect the-- the camp. And the German will show how wonderful we live here, that we have-- play soccer.

We have-- went in some barracks, which they designated, the German, to take them there, show them how-- evidently the-- the Swiss Red Cross must have got some complaints that was happening here. And they want to show-- they wouldn't take them to Birkenau, show them the crematoriums.

INT: All right, Mark, we're going to continue on with this in just a moment.

MS: OK.CREW: --this is roll number five.

INT: Mark, in the summer of 1944, the Red Cross came to visit?

MS: Yeah. I-- I-- I didn't know at the time--

INT: Swiss Red Cross.

MS: But I found out later that a delegation from the Swiss Red Cross came to visit.

INT: Did-- they-- they spruced up the camp and made things look good.

MS: Yeah.

INT: Did conditions remain good for you after that?

MS: No. This was only for one day, as long as--

INT: One day.

MS: As long the delegation came to visit.

INT: How long were you in Auschwitz?

MS: Well, I arrived someplace in '43. I was there till the camp was evacuated. That means prior to evacuation, I would say sometimes in the-- in the fall of '44, one day, we were told that we have to lined up in appell, and we're going to march someplace. We didn't know where. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

We-- in front of the kitchen, there was a-- a metal-- a-- a metal bar, a shape of a horseshoe. Long one. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And we start marching.

INT: And when was this?

MS: This had to be in-- in the-- in the fall of '44.

INT: In the fall of '44.

MS: Yeah.

INT: All right.

MS: And so we start marching.

INT: The entire camp left at the same time?

MS: The-- the-- the entire-- the-- the entire camp, yeah. We passed by. And-- and we hear that they're going to hang some prisoners, hang. There was-- right in front of the kitchen was that-- it's still there now. Found out later, it seems these five young Jewish girls who were working in one of those Farben factories in the camp managed to smuggle gunpowder to the prisoners in the crematorium. And the prisoners blew up one of those crematoriums.

As a punishment-- they were caught-- they were hung on that, in front of the kitchen. And all the prisoners, the whole camp, had to march by and watched that. And they each-- that this happens when you do something. I mean, a warning.

INT: Well, did the killings stop at that time, or did they continue on?

MS: Which one? What's that?

INT: The-- the crematoria.

MS: Oh, they-- they were-- they're still going.

INT: They still worked?

MS: Yeah, sure.

INT: All right. So when did you leave Auschwitz?

MS: [SIGHS] I think January-- I think it was January in '45. Middle of the night, we hear some big guns, big, big guns. I mean, noise, real noise. It must've been about 3 or 4 o'clock in the morning. And I said to the prisoners above me, you know something? I don't know why I know this. Something tells me this might be our last day in Auschwitz.

I don't know what made me say-- say that. He says, why? You hear those guns? Something is happening. I don't know whose guns were they, you know what I mean. But something-- something is happening close to us. And I was right.

The orders came out that everybody leave the barracks, assemble in assembly place, you know, I mean, at such and such an hour. And I had to-- and I had to make a final decision, myself. I managed to find a-- a-- a place which I could crawl in, being that I was skinny.

Crawl in behind a closet, which you could just enough go behind there, hide. And there was no way somebody could find me. The only way they could find me, if they have a-- a German dog come and smell me out or--

INT: Did you hide in this closet?

MS: No, no. Now, wait a minute. I was contemplating. Shall I take the chance? Because on one hand, I figured, if the Germans evacuate in the camp, we knew that we had to leave the camp, are they going to blow up the camp? The barracks? Are they going to put gas in there? Or they going to come and inspect it?

INT: So you had-- you really had a lot of problems at that point.

MS: I had to make up my mind. Of course in hindsight, I found out later on, if I stayed there, I would have saved myself. I'd have been liberated by the-- by the Russians. But at that time, I have no way of knowing.

So I decided, no, it's too much of a chance, I think. I figured the Germans will not leave those-- that evidence here. They're not going to let-- let prisoners stay in the-- in the barracks, you know. They either throw some gas bombs or-- or blow it up, whatever it is. So I decided to go on the-- this was the-- this is famous death march.

INT: Describe the conditions on that death march. What was the weather like?

MS: Was cold, snow. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] We couldn't go on this-- on the-- on the primary roads, because the Germans' army was retreating on the main highways. We had to go through a path of snow up to here, up to our knees, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] just the thin clothes you had on you.

INT: How did you march? How were you lined up?

MS: Lined up, I think about four or five across, soldiers, German soldiers on both sides, with dogs. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And every few minutes, you hear a shot. I was wondering what's happening in the end. We found out that if anybody fell down, couldn't walk anymore, the-- the last contingency, shot them on the spot.

INT: How long did this death march last?

MS: [SIGHS] Had to be at least two days.

INT: Were you given any food, absolutely.

MS: No.

INT: Were you able to rest along the way?

MS: The only time, we rest one time, because we-- we-- it was the middle of the night. There are some Russian planes over, flying, and throwing some flares. So we were told to lie down flat on the-- on the snow.

Because they want to see-- see what-- they were looking for the German Army retreating. But we were in the same vicinity, because the main roads were here. We were right next to them. Because we could hear the German Army retreating.

So we're going. And we see men on both sides, falling apart-- falling down, falling down, falling down. They couldn't make it anymore. And I started to hallucinate. I got tired, sleepy, hungry, and cold. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

And I didn't know where I was anymore. All I know is I wanted to sleep. I was thinking I'm going to lay down a bit, sleeping at night. Just fell down on the-- on the thing.

The next thing I feel is that somebody is hitting me with the butt of the gun. German soldier went over and hit me with the gun. Get up, get up. Something like that. Fortunately for me, there were two prisoners, evidently saw me, that I was that young, went and grabbed me and-- and grabbed me by both arms and dragged me for a while, till I came to.

INT: So people did help each other if they were able to?

MS: I don't know. Maybe these two-- two people-- I found out later it was a-- a father and a son. And they saw me being a-- a young kid. Evidently, they-- they felt pity for me, because they figured, if I don't get up, the guy is going to shoot me.

INT: All right--

MS: And we watched, till we got to Gleiwitz, which is-- which is of-- crossed the-- the-- the German border. Put us on open cattle trains. And from Gleiwitz, they took us to Buchenwald.

INT: What were the conditions like in Buchenwald?

MS: Terrible.

INT: Was there sickness there?

MS: Sickness, hunger. We were playing-- playing hide and seek, because we knew that the war was coming to an end. And with the crematoria were still going.

INT: When did you arrive in Buchenwald? Do you know?

MS: It had to be sometimes in '45. Because when we left Auschwitz, it was January 1945.

INT: Right.

MS: And we were liberated April '45.

INT: So you were in--

MS: So it had to be sometimes in-- in January or the beginning of February.

INT: All right. Were you given food in Buchenwald?

MS: In Buchenwald, we-- we got a-- a little soup, yeah.

INT: Did you do any work at that time?

MS: They-- work, yes. They did some work cleaning up. We had to pick up the dead, you know, working menial jobs. But basically we were hiding. Because the-- some of the German guards were fleeing, and they left some older German guards to watch us. And the-- of course, the crematorium was still going, and the dead people were still dying.

So we came to one barrack. We used to-- especially kids my age used to hide here that we shouldn't get caught. Because we knew that the war is coming to an end, because we-- we heard that guns from the American Army.

INT: So you knew that things were happening?

MS: Yeah. And we figured if we can hold out a little longer--

INT: And when-- when were you liberated?

MS: April 11. The-- the-- the-- the army came in. The French and-- and American soldiers came in.

INT: What happened to the guards?

MS: The ones who remained were some old German guards. And of course, they were taken prisoners. And then some of them were beaten up by the-- by the prisoners, but they were protected by the Americans, [INAUDIBLE].

INT: What was your physical condition at the time of liberation?

MS: Almost like a skeleton.

INT: How much did you weigh?

MS: [LAUGHS] Don't ask me. I wouldn't know.

INT: You did not know?

MS: I-- I didn't bother to look myself, either. Because I could feel my bones all over the place.

INT: How-- what happened when they saw how sick you were? How did they treat you?

MS: Well, what happened was the-- the Americans came in with food. And that was the worst thing they could do. Because the people were so hungry, they overate. And they-- being that hungry, some of them died after liberation for overeating, which they-- their system couldn't-- couldn't-- couldn't absorb.

INT: Did they have medical care?

MS: They had the ambulances and put them on-- on trucks, bring in-- tried to save as many as possible, put them on-- on-- on-- on trucks to bring them to some field hospitals.

INT: Where were you sent?

MS: I was-- I was still in the barracks. But I was given some-- some rations, [LAUGHS] you know. It's the funniest thing, you know. The American soldiers gave us some white bread, and I was-- I thought it was cake. I'd never seen such white bread, you know, in the package.

So we ate, you know, and come to it. And we stayed there for-- for about a week or two. And this-- this Jewish chaplain comes in. Of course, later on, is the Rabbi Herschel Schachter, and he was speaking Yiddish. And, you know, we were glad to see somebody, a-- a-- a couple of Jewish soldiers who spoke Yiddish, you know, and they told us they're Jewish and everything else.

So-- and-- and he says, stick around here because we're going to try to get you out of here. I mean, and he organized this-- this train.

INT: Did you ever go back? Did you want to go back to Wielun?

MS: No. No desire. Because I knew I had nobody there. My mother and sister were in Auschwitz. And-- and most of the-- the city was-- there was no Jews there. My father was dead. I knew that.

INT: So where did you go at that point?

MS: I-- I had no desire to go back to Poland. I, from there, the Rabbi Schachter organized the train and took all the kids up to-- I think up to 18 years old or 17 years old, put us on a train, sent us to-- to Switzerland.

INT: And you recuperated in Switzerland?

MS: And-- and-- and the sanatoriums-- sent us to the mountains. I-- I wound up in a-- in a-- in a Swiss hospital. I-- I-- my-- my skin was scabby all over. My-- I had rashes all over because from hunger-- no food, and you know.

INT: Poor nutrition.

MS: Yeah.

INT: And how long did you stay in Switzerland?

MS: We stayed in-- in that-- in the mountain till we recovered, for at least about two or three months. And after that--

INT: And when did you leave Switzerland?

MS: Well, I stayed from-- from 19-- this was 1940-- 1945, we arrived to Switzerland, and it was the end of '45. And I stayed-- stayed till 1947, when I received my paper from my-- from my uncle in the United States to come to the States.

INT: And where did you come when you came to the United States?

MS: Came to New York.

INT: And what was your occupation? What did you do at that time?

MS: I had no occupation. I just took any available job possible. I-- I was a floor boy in a-- in a factory, sweeping the floor. I was working in a place to make hamburgers, anything to support myself.

And if I was working during the day, I was going to night school. And eventually, I wound-- wound up being a furrier, because that's what my uncle did. He was a-- he was-- he was not well-off, he was just a-- a worker. And he talked to his-- his-- his boss to-- to give me a job. I started from scratch, like a floor boy, shipping clerk and everything.

INT: What happens in 1950?

MS: And in 1950, the war-- the Korean War broke out, and I got a draft notice. And I-- I went down to the-- to the office where they get the draft notice, and I said, what is this, here? They said, you apply for the first paper to come-- to become a citizen of the United States. So you have to register for the draft.

I said, but I'm not even a citizen. Says, doesn't matter. You-- you want to become a citizen? I said yes. And you-- you apply for first papers. I said yes. And then besides, he says, if you go to-- if you go to the army, you become a citizen much sooner. So I was drafted in November 1950, and I was discharged in November 1952.

INT: And you became a citizen?

MS: Yes.

INT: OK. Did you marry after the war?

MS: After I-- after I came out from the Korean War, I-- I married, got married, yes.

INT: And do you have any children?

MS: I have two sons.

INT: Did-- have you spoken of the Holocaust to your children?

MS: Not when they were young, when they got older. Eventually, little by little, little, we-- I-- up to the time when I felt that they will understand or comprehend, because they-- they were kids.

They would ask me-- ask me about my number. And I told them it was a telephone number. You know, I tried to evade it. Because I felt it-- it would be hard for them to understand to such time till they're old enough for me to tell them.

INT: Is there any message that you would like to send to future generations or to your children?

MS: I had-- I had the occasion last year to participate at March of the Living. So I was asked if I would like to accompany these teenagers, mostly here from-- from Florida now, which was Boca Raton and-- and Delray. At first I-- I hesitated, because I felt I have no reason to go back. Only got bad memories there. That's the last place where I saw my mother. And I had no relatives or anybody.

But after considering for a while, I felt I had obligation to-- to-- to my loved ones and whoever perished, as the survivors are getting older, and somebody has to remember what was happening here 50 years ago. And what better way is to accompany teenagers, especially high school kids, so some of them going to the college, and show them the place where I lived, where I arrived, and the conditions. There's many-- there's a museum there, and there's a lot of artifacts still remaining. And-- and--

INT: This is in Auschwitz?

MS: Auschwitz, yes. So I had occasion-- we-- we visited a couple of camps, not just Auschwitz, but Auschwitz and then Treblinka. And we went to Majdanek.

And I spent almost-- we spent almost 10 or 12 days with these teenagers, from place to place. And I alternated different bus. Each bus held about 30 teenagers. And they asked me questions. I pointed out things to them in Auschwitz and-- and on the bus from my experiences. And I felt that I-- in any way that I contributed, I felt good about it after it was all over.

INT: Before we close, let me ask you, did you find your mother and your sister eventually?

MS: No. My mother-- I found out that my mother died in Auschwitz. Before I came to the States, I found out that my sister survived Bergen-Belsen, and she was sick. She had TB. And somehow or another, she wound up in a sanatorium in-- in Sweden till she recuperated.

Because you couldn't-- she couldn't come out to the States until her lungs were clear, because they checked on the x-rays. Once she-- she got better, after two years of me being in the States, I-- I sent over for her.

INT: Before we close, is there anything that you would like to add about your experiences that I have not asked you about?

MS: I-- I think I covered most of it. I don't know if I left anything out. There's a possibility. But I fairly covered all-- all facets of-- of-- of-- of my war years.

INT: Well, I want to thank you for sharing your experience with us, Mark.

MS: I'm glad I could have been helpful in any way.

INT: Mark, would you like to introduce this lovely lady who's sitting beside you?

MS: I've been a widower for the past five years. And I met this lovely widow a year and a half ago. And we're engaged to be married. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] And I have two sons, and she has two children. And we're going to have one big family.

And this is the happiest day of my life, for-- because I was quite miserable for the past five years, being-- living by myself. And I was fortunate and lucky to meet Gail, who is lovely. And I think we'll have a nice, happy life.

INT: Well, we certainly wish you the best of everything, both of you.

GAIL REDNER: Thank you. Thank you.

INT: We do have some photographs that we would like to include on this tape.

MS: This is a picture of my mother, my sister, and myself, sent before the war to my Uncle Szaja in the United States. At that time, this is-- I believe this was in 1935. My mother at that time was in the early 30s.

CREW: What's her name?

MS: Her name was Adele. Her maiden name was Szaja. My last-- the last name, of course, married, was Szczupak. My sister's name is [NON-ENGLISH]. [NON-ENGLISH]. She survived a concentration camp, Bergen-Belsen. I brought her over here in 1949. She passed away a couple of years in California. She has two children and two grandchildren-- three grandchildren. And then on the bottom is myself, Moishe Szczupak, which my name is Mark Shupak now.

This is my uncle. His name was Avrum [NON-ENGLISH] Szaja. He was a brother of my mother's. This is his wife, [? Sasha. ?] And their little child's name is Hannah. She was named after my grandmother who died before the war.

My uncle perished during the Second World War. I don't know exactly where, but he was with me in the Lódz ghetto, and then he was taken away someplace, and I never-- haven't seen him since. So I don't believe any of them survived.

INT: When was this photograph taken and where?

MS: This picture was taken before the war, I believe in 1937, in Wielun, Poland, in the park, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] adjacent to the synagogue.

This is the synagogue from our hometown in Wielun. It was built in 1840 by Italian architect. Inside, it was the beautiful dome. The ceiling was a-- a-- beautiful pictures on the dome. The reason I remember this synagogue because as a child, prior to the Second World War, I was singing in the choir for Jewish holidays. September 1, 1939, it was destroyed by the Germans.

And right now I-- I happened to go last year to my hometown for a short visit, and there is no trace of it ever existed. And there is no Jews who live in my hometown either. So there was no trace of any Jews, any monuments, or any kind of life ever existed of Jews in my hometown.

In 1942, 20 Jews were picked up at random. Among them was my father. They were held incommunicado for about two days. On the third day, there were gallows built in the marketplace.

The 20 Jews were marched to the marketplace, among them my father. And they selected 10 Jews to put the hangman nooses on the other 10 Jews. And these were hanging for a-- the past-- for a whole day. At the end of the day, they were cut down, and they were buried in the Jewish cemetery. And some place in the crowd, I am standing and watching this.

This is the picture of the 10 Jews who were hung. If you noticed, they're dangling down from the hangman's noose. They were-- they were whole day there, for about five, six hours in the coldest day of the year. And by the time we cut them down, they were frozen stiff, like boards.

And if you noticed, there is nobody there anymore, because they were just hanging for everybody who passes by to see. And eventually, they were cut down and buried. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And I don't know how this picture came into being, because I never knew that ever existed.

Evidently, either a German took the picture, or somebody in the crowd. Not Jews, certainly not Jews, because the Germans would have killed them if they saw anybody taking that picture. My-- but the possibility is that some German took that.

This is a picture of myself and my friend, fellow survivor. It was taken in 1949 in Brooklyn. We were dressed up to go to a dance. So we all dressed up in ties and shirts. And I'm sorry to say my friend passed away since then.

INT: What is your friend's name?

MS: My name-- my friend's name was Joe Glassman.

CREW: And which one is he?

MS: He's the shorter one.

CREW: And the taller one is?

MS: Is me.

CREW: And you are?

MS: Mark Shupak.

This is me. The picture was taken in January, in 1951. I was drafted November 1950 in the Korean War. I just finished my basic training, and it was my first leave to come to New York. And that's where the picture was taken, in the studio of my friend's father.

CREW: And you are?

MS: And my name is Mark Shupak.

This is my oldest son, Neal Shupak. He is 38 years old. He lives-- resides in Smithtown, Long Island. And he is in business right now. He went to University of Albany, and he went into business, and he's still in business in Long Island.

This is my youngest son, Steven. He was named after my father. He's 34 years old. He lives in Brooklyn, New York, and he works at Cabrini Hospital, downtown New York. And I expect him pretty soon. My two sons alternate coming here. They met Gail, and they're very happy to have met her. So.

This is a picture of my future bride. Her name is Gail Redner, and myself, Mark Shupak. We hope to be married in the next two years. She is a widow, as-- and I'm a widower, and we're happy to have met each other. And you noticed we are quite happy in this occasion. We have went to a celebration, a party.

This is a picture of teenagers from Boca Raton, Florida, who participated last year at the March of the Living. I was asked to accompany them, and I was glad to go. And this-- you-- if you notice, I am right in the middle of the picture.

After we left Europe, Auschwitz and Majdanek and Treblinka, we flew to Israel. After dreadful scenes, these kids were glad to finally arrive in Israel. And-- and this is right in front of the wall in Jerusalem.

This-- you see the picture of myself? This was in April 1994. It was taken in Jerusalem, right in front of the wall. And you can see of-- in my smile that I was glad that I'm back on Jewish soil after going through these terrible countries in Europe, where so many of my relatives and Jews perished. So all the children were glad to get out of-- of hell, I could say, and be back on-- in the Holy Land.

This is a picture taken with my fiance in the Fontainebleau in Miami. The gathering of survivors, we celebrated our 50th year of-- of liberation. And there was a two-day celebration. We met our old friends, made some new friends. All of them were survivors.

And we were reminiscent about our past. And we were glad to see each other, because we don't know how long you'll be able to see them. After 50 years, some of us are disappearing. And so we were glad that we had a-- a last occasion to see each other. And it was a very happy occasion.

